<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Big Five (Hawaii)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Big Five were five corporations that dominated the economy and politics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> in the late 19th and early 20th centuries: Castle &amp; Cooke, Alexander &amp; Baldwin, C. Brewer &amp; Co., American Factors and Theo H. Davies &amp; Co. Founded as agents for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations, they also controlled shipping, banking and trade. Their power declined after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> as unions and the Democratic Party grew stronger.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/big-five-hawaii.jpg" alt="Big Five (Hawaii)" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: joanna orpia from QLD, australia · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Puunene_mill.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Commercial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> production in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> began in the first years of the 19th century but stayed minor until the Reciprocity Treaty of 1875 allowed duty-free trade in sugar between the Kingdom of Hawaii and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Plantations grew and merged, falling in number from 79 in 1875 to 20 in 1883. The agencies had served mainly to give credit against future sales, ship the crop and buy equipment for an industry whose growing season ran 18 to 24 months; now plantations expanding onto marginal land depended on them for capital.</p><p>By 1889 C. Brewer &amp; Co., H. Hackfeld &amp; Co., Castle &amp; Cooke and Theo H. Davies &amp; Co. controlled 56 per cent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>'s sugar crop. The name Big Five was also applied to the missionary families from whom most of the firms came, and by 1920 the second generation of missionary descendants controlled all five.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The firms' politics ranged from support for the monarchy to support for revolution, but children of some partners sat on the Committee of Safety, which organised the overthrow of the Hawaiian Kingdom and pressed for annexation. Annexation by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> removed tariffs on Hawaiian sugar, and the five companies grew into conglomerates controlling 90 per cent of the sugar business. They also held shipping lines, utilities, railroads, banks and insurance firms, sat on one another's boards, kept prices high and generally backed the Republican Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>; territorial governors often had ties to them. In 1903 Attorney General Edmund Pearson Dole compared the territory's centralised government with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>.</p><p>Organised labour made them a target. A Labor Day parade on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> in September 1949 carried a sign calling on Congress to investigate the Big Five, and in the Democratic Revolution of 1954 the unions dealt them a decisive blow.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> industry declined after statehood in 1959, so did the companies. The US Department of Justice challenged the joint ownership of the Matson Navigation Company by four of them, and Alexander &amp; Baldwin bought out the others' stakes in 1964. Jardine Matheson bought Theo H. Davies in 1973, C. Brewer was taken over in 1978 and liquidated from 2001, Castle &amp; Cooke merged with David H. Murdock's Flexi-Van Corporation in 1985, and H. Hackfeld, later Amfac, went bankrupt in 2002. Alexander &amp; Baldwin diversified and remained in business.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Big Five (Hawaii)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Five corporations, the <b>Big Five</b>, controlled sugar, shipping and banking, while James Dole made the islands the world's leading pineapple producer.</blockquote><a href="/state/territory-of-hawaii">Territory of Hawaii</a></li></ul></section>
